(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Sleep-disordered breathing is divided into obstructive sleep apnea, central sleep apnea and which third category?","choices":["Periodic limb movement disorder","Mixed apnea with resumed effort","Nighttime reflux disease","Sleep-related hypoventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sleep-disordered breathing is divided into three categories: obstructive sleep apnea, central sleep apnea and sleep-related hypoventilation. Hypoventilation during sleep reappears in obesity hypoventilation syndrome, where that subgroup is treated with bilevel positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 10, 19, 33"},{"q":"Which test is the gold standard for diagnosing obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Polysomnogram","At-home testing kit","Screening questionnaire","Arterial blood gas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A polysomnogram is the gold standard, whether run as a split-night study (2 hours of baseline, then pressure titration) or recorded through the whole night. It yields the apnea-hypopnea and respiratory disturbance indices used to confirm the diagnosis. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 16, 32"},{"q":"Which metabolic condition is more prevalent in patients with obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Hyperthyroidism","Lower triglyceride levels","Improved insulin sensitivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with obstructive sleep apnea have an increased prevalence of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes mellitus and diabetes complications. The disorder is independently associated with higher glucose and triglycerides and may exacerbate cardiovascular risk in metabolic syndrome. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 15, 20"},{"q":"Pulmonary hypertension from obstructive sleep apnea is especially likely when the apnea coexists with which condition?","choices":["Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Reflux disease","Allergic rhinitis","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive sleep apnea is classified with group 3 pulmonary hypertension, especially when it coexists with obesity hypoventilation syndrome or another cause of daytime hypoxemia. Right-sided heart failure from pulmonary hypertension is a late feature of obesity hypoventilation. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 19, 20, 30"},{"q":"Which pair of cardiovascular effects accompanies untreated obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Steadier blood pressure, fewer arrhythmias","Lower risk of aortic dissection","More sympathetic tone and arrhythmias","Less sympathetic tone, fewer arrhythmias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea increases sympathetic tone and arrhythmias, and also brings bradycardia, more variable blood pressure and a higher risk of aortic aneurysms and dissections. Arrhythmias and ischemic heart disease are morbidities of untreated disease. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 21"},{"q":"Which finding in an obese patient should prompt screening for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Low serum bicarbonate","Enlarged tonsils","Morning headaches","Facial plethora"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Facial plethora (facial swelling, puffiness) is one of the features that should prompt screening in an obese patient, with unexplained low oxygen saturation, unexplained exertional dyspnea, signs of pulmonary hypertension or right-sided heart failure, and raised serum bicarbonate. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 31, 32"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, what neck size scores a point?","choices":["14 inches or larger","15 inches or larger","16 inches or larger","17 inches or larger"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The neck item asks whether the shirt collar is 16 inches (40 cm) or larger, measured around the Adam's apple, and it scores the same for men and women. With 2 or more of the first four questions, a 16-inch neck also places a person at high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 17"},{"q":"Which feature is less common in central sleep apnea than in obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Snoring","Daytime sleepiness","Insomnia","Nocturnal dyspnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Snoring is less common in central sleep apnea than in obstructive sleep apnea, where loud, habitual snoring is typical. Otherwise the two sound very similar: insomnia, frequent awakenings, nocturnal dyspnea, nonrestorative sleep and daytime sleepiness. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 10, 25"},{"q":"In heart failure, what causes the Cheyne-Stokes breathing that predisposes to central sleep apnea?","choices":["Upper airway collapse in sleep","Reduced inspiratory muscle strength","Low sensitivity to carbon dioxide","Long lung-to-carotid circulation delay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In heart failure, Cheyne-Stokes breathing is caused by a prolonged circulation delay between the pulmonary capillaries and the carotid chemoreceptors. Heart failure is a risk factor for central sleep apnea especially through this pattern, which may even be protective. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 26"},{"q":"Which examination finding is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea rather than a screening feature for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Hepatomegaly","Enlarged tonsils","Facial plethora","Pedal edema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enlarged tonsils are a risk factor and examination finding of obstructive sleep apnea, alongside obesity, macroglossia, micrognathia, retrognathia, a thick neck and a crowded posterior oropharynx. They point to structural narrowing of the upper airway. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 31"},{"q":"A woman answers yes to loud snoring and to being observed to stop breathing during sleep; her body mass index is 38 kg/m2 and her shirt collar is 16 inches. She is not tired during the day, is not treated for high blood pressure and is not older than 50. What is her STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) total?","choices":["3","4","5","6"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + observed apnea 1 + body mass index above 35 1 + neck 16 inches or larger 1 = 4; tiredness, pressure, age and gender (female) score 0. A total of 4 is intermediate, but yes to 2 of the first four questions plus a body mass index above 35 makes her high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"During an obstructive apnea, what happens to respiratory effort?","choices":["It continues without airflow","It stops along with airflow","It stops, then later resumes","It alternates with hyperventilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In an obstructive apnea there is no airflow, but respiratory effort continues: the upper airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe. That persisting effort is what separates it from a central apnea, where effort is absent too. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24"},{"q":"What is known about how positive airway pressure therapy affects the cardiovascular complications of obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["It speeds their progression","Its effect is uncertain","It reliably reverses them","It prevents them in most patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is unsure how therapy affects the cardiovascular complications, even though untreated disease tends to progress to pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure. Persistent significant awake hypoxemia on positive airway pressure marks a poor prognosis. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries high mortality from which condition?","choices":["Reflux disease","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Heart failure","Gout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mortality of untreated obstructive sleep apnea includes high mortality from heart failure, a 3 to 6 times risk of sudden cardiac death at night and 2.5 times the driving accidents. Arrhythmias and ischemic heart disease add to the morbidity. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 20"},{"q":"Why does the workup for obesity hypoventilation syndrome include a toxicology screen and thyroid function tests?","choices":["To rule out other causes","To grade obstructive apnea severity","To confirm awake hypercapnia","To choose between pressure modes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion: alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation must be excluded. The workup therefore includes a metabolic panel, blood count, thyroid function tests, pulmonary function tests, chest radiograph and toxicology screen. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 32, 33"},{"q":"Which condition belongs in the differential diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Nocturnal seizures","Enlarged tonsils","Poorly controlled hypertension","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nocturnal seizures are in the differential of obstructive sleep apnea, with central sleep apnea, nocturnal dyspnea from heart failure, swallowing disorders, panic attacks, nocturnal asthma, reflux and other causes of daytime sleepiness such as narcolepsy. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 10, 18, 20"},{"q":"What defines obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Hypoventilation confined to sleep","Airway collapse despite effort","Absent drive with absent effort","Awake hypoventilation in obesity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome, also called Pickwickian syndrome, is awake alveolar hypoventilation (raised awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure) in an obese person that cannot be attributed to other conditions causing alveolar hypoventilation. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24, 28, 30"},{"q":"In obesity hypoventilation syndrome, when are medications used?","choices":["After diet and exercise fail","As first-line with pressure","Before any lifestyle change","Instead of pressure therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medications are second-line, only for patients who fail diet and exercise measures. First-line therapy is a weight loss program plus positive airway pressure, supported by diet changes, exercise and behavioral modifications. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which test is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Pulmonary function tests and chest radiograph","Polysomnography with nocturnal carbon dioxide","At-home sleep apnea testing kit","Split-night study with pressure titration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polysomnography with continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Serum bicarbonate and an arterial blood gas guide the workup before it, and other causes of hypoventilation must be excluded. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 32"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, what is the fewest yes answers that places a person at high risk by total alone?","choices":["2","3","4","5"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. By total alone, 5 to 8 yes answers mean high risk, 3 to 4 intermediate risk and 0 to 2 low risk. A lower total can still be high risk through a combination: 2 or more of the first four questions plus male gender, a body mass index above 35 or a 16-inch neck. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Above what awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is alveolar hypoventilation present for the diagnosis of obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["35 mmHg","40 mmHg","45 mmHg","50 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis needs awake alveolar hypoventilation, an arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mmHg, plus obesity (body mass index above 30) and exclusion of alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation. It is a diagnosis of exclusion. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"To whom is a patient with obesity hypoventilation syndrome referred as part of patient education?","choices":["Endocrinologist","Gastroenterologist","Sleep specialist","Cardiologist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patient education in obesity hypoventilation syndrome includes referral to a sleep specialist and proper counseling, within the multidisciplinary approach the syndrome needs. First-line care pairs a weight loss program with positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 19, 33, 34"},{"q":"Many patients with obesity hypoventilation syndrome present late with which end-stage picture?","choices":["Hypoxemic hypercapnic respiratory failure","Hyperventilation alternating with apnea","Isolated left-sided heart failure","Low carbon dioxide with normal oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Many patients present late, with end-stage features: severe hypoxemic hypercapnic respiratory failure and right-sided heart failure from pulmonary hypertension. Earlier symptoms are not specific and reflect obesity and coexisting obstructive apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 30, 31, 32"},{"q":"For which patient with obstructive sleep apnea is autotitrating positive airway pressure suited?","choices":["One with central apnea despite pressure therapy","One without significant comorbid conditions","One with significant cardiopulmonary disease","One with neuromuscular weakness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Autotitrating (adjustable) positive airway pressure is used in patients with obstructive sleep apnea who have no significant comorbid conditions. A patient with significant comorbidities falls outside that group. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 12, 26"},{"q":"Which risk modification is advised for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Take a sedative to deepen sleep","Stop nasal steroids for rhinitis","Avoid alcohol and other sedatives","Sleep supine to open the airway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Risk modification for obstructive sleep apnea means avoiding alcohol and other sedatives, together with diet and exercise for weight loss. Alcohol and sedative use is itself a risk factor for the disorder. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 11, 12, 15"},{"q":"Why is mortality from obstructive sleep apnea lower in the elderly population?","choices":["The disorder resolves with age","Older patients compensate","Prevalence falls with age","Older adults rarely snore"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mortality from obstructive sleep apnea is lower in the elderly because patients compensate. Compensation is not cure: older patients still have drowsiness and nocturia, which improve with continuous positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 22"},{"q":"Which therapy is the most effective treatment for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Nasal expiratory resistance device","Positional therapy with a sleep belt","Oral appliance worn during sleep","Continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Continuous positive airway pressure is the most effective non-pharmacologic therapy for obstructive sleep apnea. Many consequences of untreated disease, such as reflux, raised proinflammatory cytokines, and drowsiness and nocturia in the elderly, improve with it. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 19, 22"},{"q":"What adjustment applies when an elderly patient with obstructive sleep apnea is given modafinil or armodafinil?","choices":["Higher doses","No adjustment by age","A sedative instead","Lower doses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Modafinil and armodafinil are given at lower doses in the elderly population. The stimulants also carry many potential drug interactions and side effects such as headaches, nervousness or anxiety, dizziness and nausea, and need caution in cardiovascular, hepatic and psychiatric conditions. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 11, 15"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, a man answers yes to loud snoring and daytime tiredness and no to every other question except gender. Which risk group applies?","choices":["No group: no apnea was observed","High: two of the first four plus male","Intermediate: a total of 3 yes answers","Low: two symptom answers are too few"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tiredness 1 + male gender 1 = 3, which by total alone would be intermediate. But yes to 2 or more of the first four questions plus male gender is a high-risk combination, so he is high risk despite a total below 5. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Among the sex-specific risk factors for obstructive sleep apnea, what neck circumference is the threshold for men?","choices":["15 inches","16 inches","17 inches","18 inches"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. As a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, the neck circumference threshold is 17 inches or more in men and 16 inches in women, and the same cut-offs appear on physical examination. The screening questionnaire is different: it scores 16 inches for anyone. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 17"}]};
